any six from:
MP1. use a metal block of known mass (balance);
MP2. insert an electric heater and thermometer in the holes;
MP3. measure starting temperature;
MP4. switch on heater for a measured time;
MP5. measure energy supplied with a joulemeter or from V × I × t;
MP6. record the final/highest temperature;
MP7. c = ΔQ ÷ (m × ΔT);
MP8. wrap the block in insulation;
MP9. add oil to thermometer hole for good contact;
MP10. repeat and average;

any six from:
MP1. ice: particles vibrate in fixed positions; heating makes them vibrate more and temperature rises;
MP2. at 0 °C energy breaks bonds; temperature constant while melting;
MP3. liquid: particles move around each other; temperature rises;
MP4. average kinetic energy increases with temperature;
MP5. some evaporation from surface;
MP6. at 100 °C energy separates particles completely; temperature constant during boiling;
MP7. steam: particles far apart, moving fast and randomly;
MP8. internal energy increases throughout;
MP9. flat sections on a temperature-time graph;

